<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis I of Etruria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Louis of Parma · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon-parma">House of Bourbon-Parma</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis I was the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, a son of Duke Ferdinand of Parma. Under the Franco-Spanish agreements of 1800–1801, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave him the former Grand Duchy of Tuscany as the Kingdom of Etruria in exchange for his family's claim to Parma. He ruled from 1801 until his death in 1803, when his young son Charles Louis succeeded under his mother's regency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-etruria.jpg" alt="Louis I of Etruria" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Goya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luis_de_Etruria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis Francis Philibert was born on 5 July 1773, the second child and eldest son of Ferdinand, Duke of Parma, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Archduchess Maria Amalia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He and his older sister Carolina were their parents' favourites, and their father instructed them personally in religion. In 1778 he hit his head on a marble table while playing with Carolina and afterwards suffered from epilepsy, and later had chest problems.</p><p>In 1795 he went to the Spanish court to finish his education and to marry one of the daughters of Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, his first cousins. He chose Infanta Maria Luisa, and they were married at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 25 August 1795. The marriage was happy, and he grew increasingly dependent on his wife as his health declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>French troops occupied the Duchy of Parma in 1796 while Louis was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Napoleon Bonaparte, who had conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and wanted Spain as an ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, proposed to compensate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, a new state that he created from the Grand Duchy of Tuscany. This was agreed in the Treaty of Aranjuez. Louis had to receive his investiture from Napoleon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He, his wife and their son travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> incognito as the Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>, and the family reached the new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in August 1801.</p><p>In 1802 Louis and his pregnant wife went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for a double wedding of her brother Fernando and her youngest sister, and their daughter was born offshore at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. They returned in December, after news of the death of Louis's father. His health worsened, and he died in May 1803, aged twenty-nine, possibly of an epileptic crisis. His son Charles Louis succeeded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-parma">Louis II of Etruria</a> under the regency of his mother, Maria Luisa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis I of Etruria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French troops occupied Tuscany in 1799, and after the Treaty of Lunéville it was turned into the Kingdom of Etruria for <b>Louis of Bourbon-Parma</b> in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By agreements between France and Spain in 1800–1801, including the Treaty of Aranjuez, the Grand Duchy of Tuscany was taken from its Habsburg-Lorraine ruler and turned into the Kingdom of Etruria for <b>Louis of Bourbon-Parma</b>, in exchange for the Bourbon claim to the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-etruria">Kingdom of Etruria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Piacenza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=1792" aria-label="Piacenza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis I of Etruria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5583 6221h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5583 6221h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=1792">What was here in 1792? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
